(window.DETAIL_C = window.DETAIL_C || {})["mth-12-05"] = {"mth-12-05-m01":{"idea":"The length of a vector is found with Pythagoras in three dimensions.","steps":["The vector has sides x, y and z along the axes.","Use Pythagoras twice to get the diagonal of the box.","|a| = √(x² + y² + z²)."],"others":"x + y + z adds lengths instead of using squares. √(x + y + z) has no squares. x² + y² + z² leaves out the square root.","trap":"Square, add, then take the root.","answer":"√(x² + y² + z²)"},"mth-12-05-m02":{"idea":"A unit vector has length 1 and points the same way.","steps":["Divide the vector by its own length.","The result has length |a|/|a| = 1.","So the unit vector is a/|a|."],"others":"|a|/a divides a number by a vector, which is not defined. a + |a| adds a vector and a number. a × |a| makes it longer.","trap":"Divide by the length.","answer":"a/|a|"},"mth-12-05-m03":{"idea":"Collinear vectors are scalar multiples of each other.","steps":["They lie along the same line.","So one is a scalar multiple of the other: a = λb.","λ is not zero for non-zero vectors."],"others":"Equal length is not needed. a · b = 0 means perpendicular. a + b = 0 only covers vectors of equal length pointing opposite ways.","trap":"Parallel needs a multiple, not equal length.","answer":"a = λb for some non-zero scalar λ"},"mth-12-05-m04":{"idea":"The section formula for internal division in the ratio m : n.","steps":["The point is (mb + na)/(m + n).","Check with m = n: it gives (a + b)/2, the mid-point.","The point is nearer a when n is larger than m."],"others":"(ma + nb)/(m + n) swaps the ratio. The forms with m − n are for external division.","trap":"m goes with b, the far end.","answer":"(mb + na)/(m + n)"},"mth-12-05-m05":{"idea":"The mid-point is the section formula with equal weights.","steps":["Put m = n = 1 in (mb + na)/(m + n).","That gives (b + a)/2.","So the mid-point is (a + b)/2."],"others":"a + b is twice the mid-point. (a − b)/2 is half the difference. (a + b)/4 is half the mid-point.","trap":"Average of the two position vectors.","answer":"(a + b)/2"},"mth-12-05-m06":{"idea":"Direction cosines are the components of a unit vector.","steps":["l = cos α, m = cos β and n = cos γ.","They are the components of the unit vector along the line.","Its length is 1, so l² + m² + n² = 1."],"others":"l + m + n = 1 or 0 is not true in general. l² + m² + n² = 0 would mean a zero vector.","trap":"Sum of squares, not sum.","answer":"l² + m² + n² = 1"},"mth-12-05-m07":{"idea":"The dot product gives a scalar using the cosine of the angle.","steps":["a · b = |a||b| cos θ.","It is zero when the vectors are perpendicular.","It is largest when they are parallel."],"others":"|a||b| sin θ is the size of the cross product. |a||b| leaves out the angle. |a| + |b| cos θ adds instead of multiplying.","trap":"Dot: cosine. Cross: sine.","answer":"|a||b| cosθ"},"mth-12-05-m08":{"idea":"Perpendicular vectors have a zero dot product.","steps":["a · b = |a||b| cos θ = 0.","For non-zero vectors, this means cos θ = 0.","So θ = 90°."],"others":"A zero cross product means parallel vectors. Equal magnitudes tell nothing about the angle. A zero sum means opposite vectors of equal length.","trap":"Dot zero: perpendicular. Cross zero: parallel.","answer":"their dot product is zero"},"mth-12-05-m09":{"idea":"The scalar projection of a on b is the length of a in the direction of b.","steps":["The projection is |a| cos θ.","Since a · b = |a||b| cos θ, this is (a · b)/|b|.","The vector projection is this times the unit vector along b."],"others":"Dividing by |a| gives the wrong thing. The cross product has the wrong sign and meaning. |a||b| is just the product of lengths.","trap":"Divide by the length of the vector you project onto.","answer":"(a · b)/|b|"},"mth-12-05-m10":{"idea":"A vector dotted with itself gives its length squared.","steps":["The angle between a vector and itself is 0°.","a · a = |a||a| cos 0° = |a|².","That is the square of the length."],"others":"0 would be for perpendicular vectors. |a| and 2|a| are the wrong powers.","trap":"Square of the length.","answer":"|a|²"},"mth-12-05-m11":{"idea":"The cross product's size uses the sine of the angle.","steps":["a × b is perpendicular to both a and b.","Its magnitude is |a||b| sin θ.","That is the area of the parallelogram with sides a and b."],"others":"|a||b| leaves out the angle. |a||b| cos θ is the dot product. The sum of sines is not a rule.","trap":"Cross: sine.","answer":"|a||b| sinθ"},"mth-12-05-m12":{"idea":"The cross product changes sign when the order is swapped.","steps":["a × b = −(b × a).","A vector is parallel to itself, so sin θ = 0.","So a × a = 0."],"others":"The three wrong options claim commutativity or give a × a a non-zero value.","trap":"Anti-commutative.","answer":"a × b = −(b × a), and a × a = 0"},"mth-12-05-m13":{"idea":"The magnitude of a × b is the area of the parallelogram.","steps":["Base |a|, height |b| sin θ.","Area = |a||b| sin θ = |a × b|.","A triangle with these sides has half this area."],"others":"|a · b| is a different quantity. |a| + |b| is a sum of lengths. ½|a × b| is the triangle.","trap":"Parallelogram: |a × b|. Triangle: half.","answer":"|a × b|"},"mth-12-05-m14":{"idea":"Cross products of the unit vectors follow a cyclic rule.","steps":["i × j = k, j × k = i and k × i = j (the order i, j, k, i).","The reverse order gives a minus sign.","So j × i = −k."],"others":"j × i = k contradicts anti-commutativity. i × j = −k reverses the cycle. i × j = 0 would mean i and j are parallel.","trap":"Going round i, j, k is positive.","answer":"i × j = k and j × i = −k"},"mth-12-05-m15":{"idea":"The scalar triple product is a volume.","steps":["b × c has the size of the area of the base.","Dotting with a gives base area times height.","The modulus is the volume of the parallelepiped."],"others":"It is not an angle, a vector, or the area of the parallelogram with sides b and c.","trap":"A scalar, up to a sign.","answer":"the volume of the parallelepiped with edges a, b and c (up to a sign)"},"mth-12-05-m16":{"idea":"Three vectors in one plane enclose zero volume.","steps":["If they lie in a single plane, the parallelepiped is flat.","Its volume is zero.","So a · (b × c) = 0."],"others":"a + b + c = 0 is enough but not needed. a × (b × c) = 0 is a different condition. a · b = b · c = 0 asks for perpendicularity.","trap":"Coplanar: triple product zero.","answer":"a · (b × c) = 0"},"mth-12-05-m17":{"idea":"A cyclic change in the order keeps the scalar triple product the same.","steps":["a · (b × c) = b · (c × a) = c · (a × b).","Swapping two vectors changes the sign.","The dot and cross can also be interchanged."],"others":"It is not |a||b||c|. a · (c × b) has the opposite sign. (a · b)(a · c) is a different quantity.","trap":"Cyclic: same. Swap: sign changes.","answer":"a · (b × c) = b · (c × a) = c · (a × b)"},"mth-12-05-m18":{"idea":"The vector triple product is 'BAC minus CAB'.","steps":["a × (b × c) lies in the plane of b and c.","a × (b × c) = b(a · c) − c(a · b).","That is (a · c)b − (a · b)c."],"others":"(a × b) × c is a different product. (a · b)c − (a · c)b has the wrong sign. (a · b)(a · c) is a scalar.","trap":"Remember BAC minus CAB.","answer":"(a · c)b − (a · b)c"},"mth-12-05-m19":{"idea":"Work is the dot product of force and displacement.","steps":["W = |F||d| cos θ.","Only the part of the force along the displacement does work.","So W = F · d."],"others":"F × d is a vector, but work is a scalar. F/d is not defined. |F| + |d| adds different units.","trap":"Work is a scalar.","answer":"F · d"},"mth-12-05-m20":{"idea":"Torque is the cross product of position and force.","steps":["τ = r × F.","Its size is rF sin θ.","The order matters: F × r would reverse the direction."],"others":"r · F is a scalar. F × r has the opposite direction. r + F adds unlike quantities.","trap":"r first, then F.","answer":"r × F"},"mth-12-05-m21":{"idea":"Square both sides of the condition.","steps":["|a + b|² = |a|² + |b|² + 2a · b.","|a − b|² = |a|² + |b|² − 2a · b.","Setting them equal gives a · b = 0, so the vectors are perpendicular."],"others":"Parallel vectors would give different lengths for the sum and difference. a = b gives |a − b| = 0. Equal magnitudes alone are not enough.","trap":"Equal diagonals mean a rectangle.","answer":"a and b are perpendicular"},"mth-12-05-m22":{"idea":"The cross product is perpendicular to both vectors. Divide by its length to get a unit vector.","steps":["a × b is perpendicular to both a and b.","Dividing by |a × b| makes its length 1.","The two signs give the two opposite directions."],"others":"±a/|a| lies along a. ±(a + b)/|a + b| is not perpendicular. ±(a · b)/|a · b| is just a sign.","trap":"Remember both signs.","answer":"±(a × b)/|a × b|"},"mth-12-05-m23":{"idea":"Parallel vectors have a zero cross product.","steps":["|a × b| = |a||b| sin θ.","For θ = 0° or 180°, sin θ = 0.","So a × b = 0."],"others":"a · b = 0 is for perpendicular vectors. a · b = |a||b| sin θ is not a valid identity. |a| = |b| says nothing about direction.","trap":"Cross zero means parallel.","answer":"a × b = 0"},"mth-12-05-m24":{"idea":"The cross product is perpendicular to its factors, so the dot product with one of them is zero.","steps":["a × b is perpendicular to a.","So a · (a × b) = 0.","It is also the triple product of a, a and b, which has two equal vectors and so zero volume."],"others":"|a × b|, |a|²|b| and |a||b| are not zero, but a perpendicular dot product always is.","trap":"Perpendicular means zero dot.","answer":"0"},"mth-12-05-m25":{"idea":"A tetrahedron is one sixth of the parallelepiped.","steps":["The parallelepiped on a, b and c has volume |a · (b × c)|.","It is made of 6 equal tetrahedra.","So the volume of one is (1/6)|a · (b × c)|."],"others":"|a · (b × c)| is the parallelepiped. 1/3 and 1/2 are the factors for a pyramid on a base without the right height factor.","trap":"Tetrahedron: one sixth.","answer":"(1/6)|a · (b × c)|"},"mth-12-05-m26":{"idea":"Use cos θ = (a · b)/(|a||b|).","steps":["(i + j) · (j + k) = 1.","Each vector has length √2.","cos θ = 1/(√2 × √2) = 1/2, so θ = 60°."],"others":"90° would need a zero dot product. 180° would need opposite vectors. 30° has the wrong cosine.","trap":"cos 60° = 1/2.","answer":"60°"},"mth-12-05-n01":{"idea":"Magnitude is the square root of the sum of squares.","steps":["|a| = √(3² + 4² + 12²).","= √(9 + 16 + 144) = √169 = 13."],"trap":"The minus sign vanishes when squared.","answer":"13"},"mth-12-05-n02":{"idea":"Multiply matching components and add.","steps":["(2)(1) = 2, (3)(−2) = −6 and (−1)(4) = −4.","Sum: 2 − 6 − 4.","a · b = −8."],"trap":"Keep the signs.","answer":"-8"},"mth-12-05-n03":{"idea":"Use cos θ = (a · b)/(|a||b|).","steps":["(i + j) · (j + k) = 0 + 1 + 0 = 1.","|i + j| = |j + k| = √2.","cos θ = 1/2, so θ = 60.0°."],"trap":"cos 60° = 0.5.","answer":"60.0°"},"mth-12-05-n04":{"idea":"Find the cross product components, then the magnitude.","steps":["a × b = (2·2 − 3·(−1), 3·3 − 1·2, 1·(−1) − 2·3) = (7, 7, −7).","|a × b| = √(49 + 49 + 49) = √147.","= 7√3 = 12.12."],"trap":"Use the determinant pattern carefully.","answer":"12.12"},"mth-12-05-n05":{"idea":"Area of a triangle is half the magnitude of the cross product of two sides.","steps":["AB = (−1, 2, 0) and AC = (−1, 0, 3).","AB × AC = (6, 3, 2), with magnitude √(36 + 9 + 4) = 7.","Area = ½ × 7 = 3.5."],"trap":"Triangle: half of the parallelogram.","answer":"3.5"},"mth-12-05-n06":{"idea":"Find b × c first, then dot with a.","steps":["b × c = (j + 4k) × (5i + 6j) = (−24, 20, −5).","a · (b × c) = 1(−24) + 2(20) + 3(−5).","= −24 + 40 − 15 = 1."],"trap":"Check with c · (a × b) = 1.","answer":"1"},"mth-12-05-n07":{"idea":"Scalar projection is (a · b)/|b|.","steps":["a · b = 2 + 6 + 12 = 20.","|b| = √(1 + 4 + 4) = 3.","Projection = 20/3 = 6.67."],"trap":"Divide by the length of b.","answer":"6.67"},"mth-12-05-n08":{"idea":"Use the internal section formula with m : n = 1 : 2.","steps":["P = (mB + nA)/(m + n) = (B + 2A)/3.","= ((7 + 2)/3, (8 + 4)/3, (9 + 6)/3) = (3, 4, 5).","The sum of the coordinates is 12."],"trap":"m goes with the far end.","answer":"12"},"mth-12-05-n09":{"idea":"Work is the dot product of the force and the displacement.","steps":["Displacement d = (4 − 1, 3 − 1, 2 − 1) = (3, 2, 1).","W = F · d = 2(3) + (−1)(2) + 3(1).","= 6 − 2 + 3 = 7 J."],"trap":"Displacement = end minus start.","answer":"7"},"mth-12-05-n10":{"idea":"Perpendicular vectors have a zero dot product.","steps":["(1)(3) + (−2)(4) + λ(−1) = 0.","3 − 8 − λ = 0, so λ = −5."],"trap":"Do not lose the sign on the last term.","answer":"-5"}};
